#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932 :topic/id #uuid "cd2e9db4-6727-5887-90bc-20eb0a0bf6d2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059862 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059441 :question/id #uuid "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065" :question/math-academy-id "e-138" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^3$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3x^{2} \\\\\n&= 18x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059442 :question/id #uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" :question/math-academy-id "e-139" :question/problem "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059443 :question/id #uuid "72830b06-6333-5b61-8257-3967312bd178" :question/math-academy-id "e-140" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = x^4 -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} + x$." :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - x^{-2} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} - (-2)x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + 2x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3469bdd-a201-5219-a734-ed3caa6b039a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059444 :question/id #uuid "acfa9025-f984-5b09-9e24-f72707ad67ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-141" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ for $y= 2x^3 + 6\\sqrt x$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we convert the radical into a rational exponent, and we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3} + 6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) + 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3x^{(3 - 1)} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{(1/2 - 1)} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + 3 \\cdot x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :topic/math-academy-id 278 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429224 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3739->437. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049782} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "3ef53278-7672-5742-99fe-d07cf78639fe" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179509 :question/id #uuid "0346b5d4-3d16-53e5-a25d-4f770ef5f863" :question/math-academy-id "e-5127" :question/problem "Write $2 + \\dfrac{2}{k}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that\n\n$$\n2=\\dfrac 2 1\n$$\n\nwe can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 1 + \\dfrac{2}{k}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $k$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{1} + \\frac{2}{k} &= \\frac{k}{k} \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} + \\frac{2}{k} \\\\ &= \\frac{2k}{k} + \\frac{2}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2k}{k} + \\frac{2}{k} &= \\frac{2k + 2}{k} \\\\ &= (2(k + 1))/(k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179510 :question/id #uuid "866c1453-6d54-584c-ae65-0bc495ebf23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5130" :question/problem "Write $\\dfrac{1}{q} + \\dfrac{1}{p+1}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $(p+1)q$, since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $p+1$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{q} + \\frac{1}{p + 1} &= \\frac{p + 1}{p + 1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{q} + \\frac{q}{q} \\cdot \\frac{1}{p + 1} \\\\ &= (p + 1)/((p + 1)q) + (q)/((p + 1)q) \\\\ &= (p + q + 1)/((p + 1)q)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3739 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050640}] :topic/title "Adding Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominator"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749 :topic/id #uuid "637f4c0b-d733-5c82-bade-e519d631ab5d" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133496 :question/id #uuid "fc3055a3-5118-5a5b-8150-caadbfdae02b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12788" :question/problem "What is the reciprocal of $-21?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the reciprocal of $-21,$ we divide $1$ by $-21.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n1 \\div (-21) = -\\dfrac{1}{21}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10b6f888-d49e-5ddf-be39-e26d7f82c110" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of an Integer"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133497 :question/id #uuid "2132b7c8-2d11-51c8-a1e3-06bb59c08f84" :question/math-academy-id "e-12789" :question/problem "Find the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{1}{10}.$" :question/worked-solution "We flip the numerator and the denominator. So, the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ is  $\\dfrac{10}{1} = 10.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b955f390-c260-5f97-8e5c-62b8eb67242e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Fraction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133960 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133498 :question/id #uuid "e757528d-e87f-5739-85d4-0b35267db955" :question/math-academy-id "e-12790" :question/problem "Calculate the reciprocal of $-2\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}=-\\dfrac{2\\cdot 3+1}{3}=-\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we flip the numerator and the denominator, keeping the negative sign. Therefore, the reciprocal of $-2\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}$ is $-\\dfrac{3}{7}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e422f35-9db3-5969-b72e-ea8bf681da91" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Mixed Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133961 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133499 :question/id #uuid "7c2a7d91-c840-5f6a-8e9d-d17673e8cb70" :question/math-academy-id "e-12791" :question/problem "What is the reciprocal of $0.4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the decimal to a fraction. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n0.4 = \\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}}{{\\color{red}{5}}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we flip the fraction, as before. So the reciprocal of $0.4$ is $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{5}}}{{\\color{blue}{2}}} = 2.5.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e46d22-ed28-5950-8672-7926f9ceef50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reciprocal of a Decimal"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2202 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :topic/title "Reciprocals of Rational Numbers"}]}
